2026-11-13T14:41:01.775	{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JM2SRgRDnERgcWtxcfTrNC","type":"tool_result","content":"432\t#### 8.0-8 Manager Round Routine and Control\n433\t\n434\tThe standing routine every round brief points to; briefs list only the round's tasks.\n435\t\n436\t**Talking to a running round:** `fleet_ctl.py tell \"…\"` appends to `rounds/R<N>/INBOX.md`. The Manager reads it at start,\n437\tbefore making a graph and at every checkpoint; `fleet_graph run` returns rc 4 when it changes. Within a request, the\n438\thuman's message wins over the request text.\n439\t\n440\t**Two round modes** (`fleet.toml` `[manager] mode`; default `resident`). `resident`: one Manager session lives for\n441\tthe whole round and does everything below. `calls`: the Manager is started as short separate sessions - ① plan\n442\t(Start + graph planning), ② judge (graph checkpoints, follow-up graphs; repeats), ③ close (Close) - and **the\n443\tengine runs `fleet_graph.py run` between them**; each call ends its report with a `NEXT: RUN G-… | CLOSE | STOP\n444\t<reason>` line, which is all the engine reads. The routine is the same; only who waits differs.\n445\t\n446\t**Round content, projects, records.** A round is launched from the web (`라운드 실행`). Without a round-specific\n447\tbrief (`.aimem/briefs/manager-round<N>-<date>.md`) the standard brief `.aimem/briefs/manager-round-standard.md` is\n448\tused and **the round's tasks are the `NEW` requests**; with nothing to do the Manager reports and ends.\n449\t- **A project spans rounds.** Each request names a project (`project` in the request file; empty = the fleet's own\n450\t  code, changed in place). Deliverables accumulate in **`projects/<project>/`** across rounds - one place, edited\n451\t  in place, no per-round copies. `projects/<project>/PROGRESS.md` is the project's memory: the Manager reads it at\n452\t  round start and prepends `## R<N>` (done, changed files, verification, **what remains**) at round close. Its\n453\t  top section `## 목표` is the project's goal checklist (`- [ ]` / `- [x]`): the Manager predicts the goals needed\n454\t  to finish the project on its first round and keeps the checks true every round - the web progress is\n455\t  done/total of that list (request average until the list exists). A new\n456\t  project gets a `README.md`. Assign paths to a slot with `aimem_db.py ownership set` before a worker writes\n457\t- **A round folder holds records only:** `rounds/R<N>/brief.md` (the brief as sent), `round.json` (launch time,\n458\t  open requests and their projects - written by the launcher; `task set --request` / `request set` during the\n459\t  round add theirs) and `REPORT.md` (the close report, also in the\n460\t  response body). No deliverables there; other rounds' folders are not touched\n461\t- **Identifiers stay as they are** (`8.0-3-1`); project and round are not prefixed to them. The web Fleet tab\n462\t  shows per-project progress: goals done/total (or request average), request counts, rounds worked (`round.json` + `PROGRESS.md`),\n463\t  and the latest `PROGRESS.md` entry\n464\t\n465\t**Start** (a new session is blank)\n466\t1. `fleet_ctl.py status` · `fleet.py status` · `aimem_db.py queue list` · `request list` · `slot list` ·\n467\t   `fleet.py runs --last 10` · `aimem_db.py handoff list --last 2`, then this header, `8.0`, `Section 9`. Trim\n468\t   output with `| head` / `| tail`\n469\t2. **Roster:** slots with role `(미배정 - 관리자가 다음 라운드에 정함)` or `events(kind=slot)` → set role and remark\n470\t   with `aimem_db.py slot set --slot S<n> --role \"…\" --note \"…\"`. Harness and model are not the Manager's to change\n471\t3. **NEW requests:** within scope and caps → split into subtasks → `PLANNED` → graph. **Caps are enforced by\n472\t   `fleet_graph.py new`:** new work stops at 3 graphs and 10 tasks per round; a graph that fixes review findings or\n473\t   re-reviews those fixes passes with `--followup \"<what it follows>\"` up to 6 graphs and 18 tasks. Never skip the\n474\t   re-review to stay under the cap. Otherwise\n475\t   `PLANNED` + \"next round\", mentioned in report item (5). A notification request from `슬롯 관리(웹)` is confirm →\n476\t   assign role → `DONE`. Mid-round arrivals are judged the same way at every checkpoint\n477\t\n478\t**Graph and verify rules** (standing; the round brief repeats them in Korean)\n479\t- **Free slots build and fix; paid slots (S1, S4) judge, design and review adversarially.** A build or fix given\n480\t  to a paid slot is listed with its reason in report item (6). Never move a failed free task to a paid slot\n481\t- **Parallel graphs:** a `deps` edge only where a node reads another's output. Fix the interface (file and function\n482\t  names, input/output shapes) first and put it in each brief, so logic, UI, styles and tests can sit side by side;\n483\t  spread one level over different slots (different providers run at the same time); reviews go last.\n484\t  `fleet_graph.py new` prints the graph's shape and notes a single-file chain or a crowded slot\n485\t- **Small nodes:** one new file, about 200 lines, about 10 checks. Split larger work; split a file two slots\n486\t  would both write. Do not tell a worker to \"plan first\" - have it edit files right away\n487\t- **The engine runs `verify` confined** (bubblewrap): writable only in the repo and `/tmp`, `.git` read-only,\n488\t  `.env` and `.aimem/tls/` unreadable, no API keys, tokens or session markers in the environment, its own pid\n489\t  namespace; the network stays. A `verify` that writes elsewhere, reads keys or signals processes fails. Without\n490\t  bubblewrap the graph log says `검증 명령이 울타리 없이 돕니다`\n491\t- **When the Manager ends** (or a calls-mode call fails) the engine halts that round's workers that have no runner\n492\t  - node back to `todo`, attempt in `stopped_attempts`, not counted - before writing the minimal close. Workers of\n493\t  other rounds and hand-started ones are untouched; nothing is halted while paused\n494\t- **`verify` asks whether the worker did the work,** by exit code, never by grepping piped output\n495\t  (`… > /tmp/x.txt 2>&1 && …`). A node that writes checks is verified by \"it ran and the number of checks grew\",\n496\t  not \"the product passed\"; a product defect is reported `NEEDS-FIX: …` and fixed by a later node whose `verify`\n497\t  is \"all pass\". Run the `verify` command on the current tree once before using it\n498\t- **The engine fails an attempt that did nothing:** a report ending in a textual tool call (`aborted`) or a first\n499\t  attempt that touched none of its `write` paths (`no-op`)\n500\t- **Screen checks ask what is rendered** (`is_visible`, computed style, bounding boxes), not attributes; a screen\n501\t  behind login gets a check with auth on. Projects with a screen check overlap and overflow after input\n502\t- **Review copies of the repo exclude `.aimem/tls/`** and are removed by the reviewer when done\n503\t\n504\t**Graph checkpoints** (after each graph, so a cut session can resume from the DB)\n505\t- (a) `aimem_db.py task set --id T-xxx --status REVIEW|DONE` to match results\n506\t- (b) one `aimem_db.py event` line: \"G-xxx finished: per-node results, what remains\"\n507\t- (c) queue update (`queue drop`/`add`)\n508\t- (d) **close `REVIEW`**: reviewed task → DONE when the cross-review node is DONE; a request → DONE only after all\n509\t  its subtasks (closed requests take progress and ETA from the request status)\n510\t- (e) re-examine newly arrived `NEW` requests by start step 3\n511\t\n512\t**Close**\n513\t1. Full tests and linter; `aimem_db.py handoff add --bump --expect-rev N --body \"**REV N+1. …**\"` (≤1,200 chars);\n514\t   `render`; new rules registered in `8.0`. **`--expect-rev` is always given** - N is the REV this session last\n515\t   saw. A refusal means another session wrote meanwhile: re-read `handoff list --last 2` and the header, then\n516\t   decide; never retry with the new number blindly\n517\t2. `python3 tools/aimem_db.py archive` once (counts in the report), then `aimem_db.py migrate && render`\n518\t3. Report: `Status: DONE|PARTIAL|BLOCKED` + (1) task table (2) reproduced verification (3) waiting time (4) queue\n519\t   progress (5) next round's plan (6) operating metrics - slot utilization, Manager-only intervals, retries\n520\t   (7) one improvement proposal. These two are a standing duty. **No commit or push** (Claude Code does it)\n521\t\n522\t**Request lifecycle:** `NEW` (web/phone form) → `PLANNED` (Manager splits) → `IN-PROGRESS` (graph) →\n523\t`DONE`/`REJECTED` (Manager). Taeseok can edit title and body only while `NEW`.\n524\t\n525\t**Control table** - who changes what\n526\t\n527\t| Target | Changed by | Path | How the Manager learns |\n528\t| ------ | ---------- | ---- | ---------------------- |\n529\t| Slot harness and model | Taeseok | web Fleet tab / `aimem_db.py slot` | auto NEW request from `슬롯 관리(웹)`, readiness check, `events(kind=slot)` |\n530\t| Slot role, remark, assignment | Manager only | `aimem_db.py slot set`, graphs | (the web refuses with 400) |\n531\t| Request creation, editing while NEW | Taeseok | request form | start step 3 / checkpoint (e) |\n532\t| Request splitting and status | Manager | `aimem_db.py request set`, graphs | - |\n533\t| Archive run | Manager (close 2) / Taeseok (web) | `aimem_db.py archive` / `POST /api/archive` | `events(kind=archive)` |\n534\t| Fleet mode (prepare, run, stop) | Taeseok | web fleet bar | `fleet_graph run` output, rc 3 when spawning is forbidden |\n535\t| Chained rounds (`이어 달리기`) | Taeseok switches it on with a round cap and picks the targets: the fleet's own work, any number of projects, or everything | web fleet bar / `fleet_ctl.py chain` | while the targets have open requests or unchecked goals, the next round is launched when one ends; it stops itself when nothing is left, the report is not DONE, a round closed no request and checked no goal, or the cap is reached |\n536\t| Commit and push | Claude Code, with Taeseok's permission | git | - |\n537\t\n538\t### 8.1 Opening Slots\n539\t\n540\t2~5 concurrent, never more. Every instruction carries slot ID, token, REV and write scope (`0.1`); a retry states\n541\twhether the previous attempt left deliverables; `label` always starts with the slot ID. **Commit order:** finish\n542\tclean-up first (board, REV, linter, handoff) so one commit is consistent - never commit an intermediate state.\n543\t\n544\t---\n545\t\n546\t## 9. Awaiting Approval (Manager → Taeseok)\n547\t\n548\tItems stopped by `8.0-4` rule 3. Approved items leave this table and are recorded in the handoff log. The Manager\n549\tnever executes an item here on its own.\n550\t\n551\t| # | Item | Why | Risk | Status |\n552\t| - | ---- | --- | ---- | ------ |\n553\t| 1 | `python3 -m playwright install firefox` | The queue's \"run the monitor's browser checks in Firefox too\" cannot start: only chromium is installed. Installing is a hard limit (`0.2`), so R12 stopped and reported instead | Downloads ~90 MB into `~/.cache/ms-playwright`; no repo or config change. Declining only means the CSP `sandbox` directive and the ticket path stay checked in chromium alone | Waiting (R12) |\n554\t| 2 | Register the status line in `~/.claude/settings.json`: `\"statusLine\": {\"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"python3 /home/tsyi/work/ai-fleet/tools/claude_usage_statusline.py\"}` | The Fleet tab's \"Claude usage\" card (R18-R19) reads `.aimem/claude-usage.json`, which only that status-line command writes. The file does not exist yet, so the card shows \"register the status-line command\" and nothing else. Writing a user-global file outside this folder is a hard limit (`0.2`), so R19 stopped and reported instead | One line in Taeseok's own Claude Code settings; the script reads nothing but the session JSON Claude Code already hands it (no credentials) and writes one file inside this folder. Declining only means the card stays empty | Waiting (R19) |\n555\t\n556\tApproved and removed items: `retired-sections-2026-11.md` `[REV 128 내림] 9절`, `[REV 135 내림] 9절 1번`; older history in\n557\t`retired-sections-2026-09.md`.\n558\t\n559\t---\n560\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad2566bf-8d41-4b46-847d-cb5a5214cf3f","uuid":"6bd31e95-a974-4a79-862d-3357f268c19f","timestamp":"2026-11-13T05:41:01.772Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/tsyi/work/ai-fleet/AIMEM.md","content":"#### 8.0-8 Manager Round Routine and Control\n\nThe standing routine every round brief points to; briefs list only the round's tasks.\n\n**Talking to a running round:** `fleet_ctl.py tell \"…\"` appends to `rounds/R<N>/INBOX.md`. The Manager reads it at start,\nbefore making a graph and at every checkpoint; `fleet_graph run` returns rc 4 when it changes. Within a request, the\nhuman's message wins over the request text.\n\n**Two round modes** (`fleet.toml` `[manager] mode`; default `resident`). `resident`: one Manager session lives for\nthe whole round and does everything below. `calls`: the Manager is started as short separate sessions - ① plan\n(Start + graph planning), ② judge (graph checkpoints, follow-up graphs; repeats), ③ close (Close) - and **the\nengine runs `fleet_graph.py run` between them**; each call ends its report with a `NEXT: RUN G-… | CLOSE | STOP\n<reason>` line, which is all the engine reads. The routine is the same; only who waits differs.\n\n**Round content, projects, records.** A round is launched from the web (`라운드 실행`). Without a round-specific\nbrief (`.aimem/briefs/manager-round<N>-<date>.md`) the standard brief `.aimem/briefs/manager-round-standard.md` is\nused and **the round's tasks are the `NEW` requests**; with nothing to do the Manager reports and ends.\n- **A project spans rounds.** Each request names a project (`project` in the request file; empty = the fleet's own\n  code, changed in place). Deliverables accumulate in **`projects/<project>/`** across rounds - one place, edited\n  in place, no per-round copies. `projects/<project>/PROGRESS.md` is the project's memory: the Manager reads it at\n  round start and prepends `## R<N>` (done, changed files, verification, **what remains**) at round close. Its\n  top section `## 목표` is the project's goal checklist (`- [ ]` / `- [x]`): the Manager predicts the goals needed\n  to finish the project on its first round and keeps the checks true every round - the web progress is\n  done/total of that list (request average until the list exists). A new\n  project gets a `README.md`. Assign paths to a slot with `aimem_db.py ownership set` before a worker writes\n- **A round folder holds records only:** `rounds/R<N>/brief.md` (the brief as sent), `round.json` (launch time,\n  open requests and their projects - written by the launcher; `task set --request` / `request set` during the\n  round add theirs) and `REPORT.md` (the close report, also in the\n  response body). No deliverables there; other rounds' folders are not touched\n- **Identifiers stay as they are** (`8.0-3-1`); project and round are not prefixed to them. The web Fleet tab\n  shows per-project progress: goals done/total (or request average), request counts, rounds worked (`round.json` + `PROGRESS.md`),\n  and the latest `PROGRESS.md` entry\n\n**Start** (a new session is blank)\n1. `fleet_ctl.py status` · `fleet.py status` · `aimem_db.py queue list` · `request list` · `slot list` ·\n   `fleet.py runs --last 10` · `aimem_db.py handoff list --last 2`, then this header, `8.0`, `Section 9`. Trim\n   output with `| head` / `| tail`\n2. **Roster:** slots with role `(미배정 - 관리자가 다음 라운드에 정함)` or `events(kind=slot)` → set role and remark\n   with `aimem_db.py slot set --slot S<n> --role \"…\" --note \"…\"`. Harness and model are not the Manager's to change\n3. **NEW requests:** within scope and caps → split into subtasks → `PLANNED` → graph. **Caps are enforced by\n   `fleet_graph.py new`:** new work stops at 3 graphs and 10 tasks per round; a graph that fixes review findings or\n   re-reviews those fixes passes with `--followup \"<what it follows>\"` up to 6 graphs and 18 tasks. Never skip the\n   re-review to stay under the cap. Otherwise\n   `PLANNED` + \"next round\", mentioned in report item (5). A notification request from `슬롯 관리(웹)` is confirm →\n   assign role → `DONE`. Mid-round arrivals are judged the same way at every checkpoint\n\n**Graph and verify rules** (standing; the round brief repeats them in Korean)\n- **Free slots build and fix; paid slots (S1, S4) judge, design and review adversarially.** A build or fix given\n  to a paid slot is listed with its reason in report item (6). Never move a failed free task to a paid slot\n- **Parallel graphs:** a `deps` edge only where a node reads another's output. Fix the interface (file and function\n  names, input/output shapes) first and put it in each brief, so logic, UI, styles and tests can sit side by side;\n  spread one level over different slots (different providers run at the same time); reviews go last.\n  `fleet_graph.py new` prints the graph's shape and notes a single-file chain or a crowded slot\n- **Small nodes:** one new file, about 200 lines, about 10 checks. Split larger work; split a file two slots\n  would both write. Do not tell a worker to \"plan first\" - have it edit files right away\n- **The engine runs `verify` confined** (bubblewrap): writable only in the repo and `/tmp`, `.git` read-only,\n  `.env` and `.aimem/tls/` unreadable, no API keys, tokens or session markers in the environment, its own pid\n  namespace; the network stays. A `verify` that writes elsewhere, reads keys or signals processes fails. Without\n  bubblewrap the graph log says `검증 명령이 울타리 없이 돕니다`\n- **When the Manager ends** (or a calls-mode call fails) the engine halts that round's workers that have no runner\n  - node back to `todo`, attempt in `stopped_attempts`, not counted - before writing the minimal close. Workers of\n  other rounds and hand-started ones are untouched; nothing is halted while paused\n- **`verify` asks whether the worker did the work,** by exit code, never by grepping piped output\n  (`… > /tmp/x.txt 2>&1 && …`). A node that writes checks is verified by \"it ran and the number of checks grew\",\n  not \"the product passed\"; a product defect is reported `NEEDS-FIX: …` and fixed by a later node whose `verify`\n  is \"all pass\". Run the `verify` command on the current tree once before using it\n- **The engine fails an attempt that did nothing:** a report ending in a textual tool call (`aborted`) or a first\n  attempt that touched none of its `write` paths (`no-op`)\n- **Screen checks ask what is rendered** (`is_visible`, computed style, bounding boxes), not attributes; a screen\n  behind login gets a check with auth on. Projects with a screen check overlap and overflow after input\n- **Review copies of the repo exclude `.aimem/tls/`** and are removed by the reviewer when done\n\n**Graph checkpoints** (after each graph, so a cut session can resume from the DB)\n- (a) `aimem_db.py task set --id T-xxx --status REVIEW|DONE` to match results\n- (b) one `aimem_db.py event` line: \"G-xxx finished: per-node results, what remains\"\n- (c) queue update (`queue drop`/`add`)\n- (d) **close `REVIEW`**: reviewed task → DONE when the cross-review node is DONE; a request → DONE only after all\n  its subtasks (closed requests take progress and ETA from the request status)\n- (e) re-examine newly arrived `NEW` requests by start step 3\n\n**Close**\n1. Full tests and linter; `aimem_db.py handoff add --bump --expect-rev N --body \"**REV N+1. …**\"` (≤1,200 chars);\n   `render`; new rules registered in `8.0`. **`--expect-rev` is always given** - N is the REV this session last\n   saw. A refusal means another session wrote meanwhile: re-read `handoff list --last 2` and the header, then\n   decide; never retry with the new number blindly\n2. `python3 tools/aimem_db.py archive` once (counts in the report), then `aimem_db.py migrate && render`\n3. Report: `Status: DONE|PARTIAL|BLOCKED` + (1) task table (2) reproduced verification (3) waiting time (4) queue\n   progress (5) next round's plan (6) operating metrics - slot utilization, Manager-only intervals, retries\n   (7) one improvement proposal. These two are a standing duty. **No commit or push** (Claude Code does it)\n\n**Request lifecycle:** `NEW` (web/phone form) → `PLANNED` (Manager splits) → `IN-PROGRESS` (graph) →\n`DONE`/`REJECTED` (Manager). Taeseok can edit title and body only while `NEW`.\n\n**Control table** - who changes what\n\n| Target | Changed by | Path | How the Manager learns |\n| ------ | ---------- | ---- | ---------------------- |\n| Slot harness and model | Taeseok | web Fleet tab / `aimem_db.py slot` | auto NEW request from `슬롯 관리(웹)`, readiness check, `events(kind=slot)` |\n| Slot role, remark, assignment | Manager only | `aimem_db.py slot set`, graphs | (the web refuses with 400) |\n| Request creation, editing while NEW | Taeseok | request form | start step 3 / checkpoint (e) |\n| Request splitting and status | Manager | `aimem_db.py request set`, graphs | - |\n| Archive run | Manager (close 2) / Taeseok (web) | `aimem_db.py archive` / `POST /api/archive` | `events(kind=archive)` |\n| Fleet mode (prepare, run, stop) | Taeseok | web fleet bar | `fleet_graph run` output, rc 3 when spawning is forbidden |\n| Chained rounds (`이어 달리기`) | Taeseok switches it on with a round cap and picks the targets: the fleet's own work, any number of projects, or everything | web fleet bar / `fleet_ctl.py chain` | while the targets have open requests or unchecked goals, the next round is launched when one ends; it stops itself when nothing is left, the report is not DONE, a round closed no request and checked no goal, or the cap is reached |\n| Commit and push | Claude Code, with Taeseok's permission | git | - |\n\n### 8.1 Opening Slots\n\n2~5 concurrent, never more. Every instruction carries slot ID, token, REV and write scope (`0.1`); a retry states\nwhether the previous attempt left deliverables; `label` always starts with the slot ID. **Commit order:** finish\nclean-up first (board, REV, linter, handoff) so one commit is consistent - never commit an intermediate state.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Awaiting Approval (Manager → Taeseok)\n\nItems stopped by `8.0-4` rule 3. Approved items leave this table and are recorded in the handoff log. The Manager\nnever executes an item here on its own.\n\n| # | Item | Why | Risk | Status |\n| - | ---- | --- | ---- | ------ |\n| 1 | `python3 -m playwright install firefox` | The queue's \"run the monitor's browser checks in Firefox too\" cannot start: only chromium is installed. Installing is a hard limit (`0.2`), so R12 stopped and reported instead | Downloads ~90 MB into `~/.cache/ms-playwright`; no repo or config change. Declining only means the CSP `sandbox` directive and the ticket path stay checked in chromium alone | Waiting (R12) |\n| 2 | Register the status line in `~/.claude/settings.json`: `\"statusLine\": {\"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"python3 /home/tsyi/work/ai-fleet/tools/claude_usage_statusline.py\"}` | The Fleet tab's \"Claude usage\" card (R18-R19) reads `.aimem/claude-usage.json`, which only that status-line command writes. The file does not exist yet, so the card shows \"register the status-line command\" and nothing else. Writing a user-global file outside this folder is a hard limit (`0.2`), so R19 stopped and reported instead | One line in Taeseok's own Claude Code settings; the script reads nothing but the session JSON Claude Code already hands it (no credentials) and writes one file inside this folder. Declining only means the card stays empty | Waiting (R19) |\n\nApproved and removed items: `retired-sections-2026-11.md` `[REV 128 내림] 9절`, `[REV 135 내림] 9절 1번`; older history in\n`retired-sections-2026-09.md`.\n\n---\n","numLines":129,"startLine":432,"totalLines":560}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JM2SRgRDnERgcWtxcfTrNC",